60,3,1,talk,Rin,,Option A is a nightmare! Imagine spending nine minutes every single morning just to get your screens working! We'd lose so much time. I think Option C is the best because we can just sit and start!,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"@Rin, I completely agree. Option A is a financial and operational disaster. The recurring costs of power adjustments and the waste of time make it untenable. Option C is the only choice that ensures long-term fiscal stability.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, while Option C is stable, it creates a permanent hierarchy. Someone will be stuck by the noisy printer and away from the light indefinitely. If we choose C, we must explicitly agree on how to assign those undesirable spots.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Mizuki, the ""hierarchy"" is a social construct; the noise and ergonomic strain are physical realities. Option A and B are illogical because they force us into a cycle of physical discomfort and constant readjustment. Option C is the only viable choice.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, @Mizuki, I see why you both like Option C! But if we group by function, will we stop talking to people in other roles? I want us to stay a close-knit team where everyone learns from everyone!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Rin,,"@Yumi, we can still talk! We just don't have to spend our morning fighting for a desk. If we have our own spots, we can use those saved minutes to chat and help each other instead!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, social connection doesn't require physical instability. Option C allows us to optimize the space once and stop wasting the budget on repeated installation fees. It is the only option that respects our financial constraints.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, I don't disagree with the budget, but we can't let ""efficiency"" be a mask for unfairness. If we go with Option C, we must decide now who takes the noisy spots, rather than letting it happen by default.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Mizuki, the solution is simple: assign seats based on who can tolerate the noise or who needs the quiet most. Forcing everyone to rotate through discomfort is not fairness; it is inefficiency. Option C is the only logical choice.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, that makes sense! If we can be thoughtful about who sits where, we can keep everyone happy. I'll support Option C as long as we make sure we're still close enough to share ideas!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Rin,,"@Yumi, exactly! We can be close and fast! Option C means no more guessing where to sit or wasting time plugging things in. It's the only way to keep our mornings smooth and our gaps free!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Rin, precisely. Any other option introduces recurring costs and operational friction. By choosing Option C, we lock in our expenses and eliminate the risk of budget overruns. It is the only responsible decision for the team's future.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, @Shion, you both prioritize logic and budget, but the most logical way to maintain a healthy team is to prevent resentment. If we choose Option C, we must commit to a transparent assignment process for the worst seats.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Mizuki, transparency is a given. However, the priority must remain the physical environment. A transparent process that still forces ergonomic instability is a failure. Option C allows us to solve the noise and comfort issues permanently. It is the only rational path.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Mizuki, I totally agree that transparency is key! If we're all honest about what we need, we can make Option C work for everyone. Let's make sure we're arranged to keep our team spirit glowing!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Rin,,I'm so glad we all agree! Option C is the winner for me because it's the fastest and most stable. No more wasting time every morning! I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,Option C is the only choice that aligns with our financial reality and ensures operational stability. It prevents recurring costs and budget waste. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I've pushed for fairness, and since everyone else is aligned on stability, I will accept it. However, we must formally agree to a transparent assignment of the noisy spots. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shion,,Option C is the only choice that respects the physical requirements of the body and the acoustic reality of the room. It eliminates the absurdity of constant readjustment. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Yumi,,"I'm so happy we found a way to make everyone feel heard! Let's use Option C to stay stable and healthy, while keeping our hearts close for all that great learning! I vote for Option C!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
